# Source manifest — reproducible-by-reference (Invariant 3).
# Re-clone at build time; the working tree under sources/mise/ is gitignored.
#
# TOOLCHAIN SOURCE — a tool this repo actually RUNS (#81, ingested 2026-08-02).
#
# ⚠️ INVARIANT: this pin MUST track the version we actually run.
#   version source: the mise binary itself (`mise --version`)
#   pinned here:    2026.9.4
# A pin that drifts from the installed version makes the corpus answer questions
# about code we are not running. Not hypothetical — `sources/graphify.manifest`
# records exactly that failure: it sat two releases behind for weeks, so every
# "how does graphify work?" answer came from the wrong source.
#
# Pin the RELEASE TAG, never a branch head — a branch drifts ahead of the release.
# To bump: change the version in its source above, resolve the tag to its COMMIT,
# set ref/commit here to match, then `mise run kb-build`:
#   gh api repos/jdx/mise/commits/<tag> --jq .sha
#
# ⚠️ NOT `git ls-remote --tags <url> <tag>` (#395), which is what this line said
# until 2026-08-19 and what put a wrong value below it. mise ships ANNOTATED tags,
# so that call returns the TAG OBJECT rather than the commit. Naming the tag
# EXACTLY is the half that makes it silent: the commit sits on the peeled
# `<tag>^{}` line, which an exact pattern does not match. Control-armed
# 2026-08-20 — `--tags <url> v2026.8.8` returns ONE line, `ca93e5fe…`, with or
# WITHOUT `--refs`; the glob `'v2026.8.8*'` returns both lines and `--refs` then
# suppresses the useful one. Neither flag rescues the exact form.
# `commit` here read
# `ca93e5fe…` from 2026-08-02 to 2026-08-19, which is the v2026.8.8 tag object
# pointing at commit `c454c6f4…`. It never surfaced because `git checkout` peels
# a tag object, so the clone was right while the recorded provenance was not.
# Control arm on the replacement call: it reproduces `sources/hk.manifest`'s pin
# byte-for-byte (hk ships LIGHTWEIGHT tags, so the two agree there) and disagreed
# here — a second route to one fact, which is how the drift was found at all.
#
# kind = code: AST extraction only, no LLM, no cost. NOTE this extracts the
# SOURCE and skips every .md by design — the tool's DOCS are a separate
# `kind = docs` mirror (#123), not this manifest.
# RESYNCED 2026-09-09 to v2026.9.4 (Ray: "resync the mise graphify sources for
# offline research"). `mise run kb-update -- mise` reported "already at latest" —
# correct and useless here: it follows the PINNED REF, and this ref is a tag, so
# it can never discover a newer release. A tag-pinned source is advanced by
# moving `ref`, never by kb-update.
#
# The commit below came from `manifest.resolve_tag(url, "2026.9.4", prefix="v")`,
# NOT from a hand `ls-remote`. Armed at the moment of the bump: the engine
# returned 794948606e02… while a hand `ls-remote refs/tags/v2026.9.4` returned
# 	d0c2938993c5… — the tag object. Different SHAs, exactly the #395 trap the
# header above describes, still live four months on.
#
# That arm matters beyond this file: on the SAME DAY, `sources/codex.manifest`
# was bumped by hand and recorded a tag object, because codex's manifest carries
# no warning like this one and the knowledge did not transfer. The engine's
# resolver has been correct since #500; only the hand path is exposed.
url = https://github.com/jdx/mise
ref = v2026.9.4
commit = 794948606e02cde091653a165291b06cae43c352
kind = code
added = 2026-08-02
